1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,416
At a paper speed of twenty-five millimetres per second, each
horizontal one-millimetre box represents zero point zero four seconds.
Five small boxes form one large box, representing zero point two
seconds. These conversions apply only after you confirm the actual
recording speed.

2
00:00:19,416 --> 00:00:37,872
At standard gain, ten vertical millimetres represent one millivolt. A
shorter calibration mark may reflect deliberately reduced gain rather
than a broken machine. Read the stated setting, verify the calibration
mark and document it before interpreting voltage or comparing
recordings.

3
00:00:37,872 --> 00:00:59,208
First choose the physiological endpoints, then count boxes. For a QRS
spanning three and a half small boxes at standard speed, multiply
three point five by zero point zero four: zero point one four seconds,
or one hundred forty milliseconds. That is a wide complex by the
school reference.

4
00:00:59,208 --> 00:01:18,072
State the unit every time. Multiplying by zero point zero four gives
seconds, not milliseconds. To express the same result in milliseconds,
multiply seconds by one thousand. This unit discipline is especially
important in the QTc calculation later in the module.

5
00:01:18,072 --> 00:01:36,528
Measure voltage vertically from a suitable baseline to the chosen peak
or nadir. Twelve millimetres at standard gain corresponds to one point
two millivolts. If gain changes, the same millimetre height represents
a different voltage, so a visual comparison alone is unreliable.

6
00:01:36,528 --> 00:01:54,408
Small waves may be harder to measure because of body habitus,
electrode contact or baseline motion. Improve the signal and repeat a
borderline measurement. Do not assign a precise amplitude to an
obscured endpoint, and do not translate voltage into the strength of
the pulse.

7
00:01:54,408 --> 00:02:16,872
For a regular rhythm at standard speed, divide three hundred by the
number of large boxes between consecutive R peaks. Four large boxes
gives seventy-five beats per minute. The landmark sequence three
hundred, one fifty, one hundred, seventy-five, sixty, fifty is the
same relationship expressed without division.

8
00:02:16,872 --> 00:02:36,840
The shortcut assumes both a known speed and a representative regular
RR interval. If intervals vary substantially, one short interval
exaggerates the average rate and one long interval underestimates it.
Examine regularity before choosing this method, even though rate is
step two in the school framework.

9
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:56,088
For an irregular rhythm, count ventricular complexes within a verified
six-second window and multiply by ten. Eight complexes gives an
estimated eighty per minute. Confirm the duration from printed time
marks or a known grid speed rather than assuming that any strip is six
seconds long.

10
00:02:56,088 --> 00:03:15,672
The general calculation is counted beats multiplied by sixty, divided
by the number of seconds sampled. Short windows give less precise
averages, especially when the rhythm varies. Report an approximate
ventricular rate and the irregularity rather than presenting the
number as an exact constant.

11
00:03:15,672 --> 00:03:35,832
Use calipers or a straight edge when helpful, and remeasure a
borderline result. Check the J point for ST displacement, not the
T-wave peak. If motion or poor contact prevents reliable measurement,
correct what you can and acquire another tracing while continuing
patient care.

12
00:03:35,832 --> 00:03:54,936
Document the setting and measurement, the limitation that affected
confidence, and whether repetition changed the finding. Do not label
every unusual number a machine fault. A correctly recorded wide QRS is
a clinical observation, whereas wrong speed assumptions are an
interpretation error.
